1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,124
Lesson 7.7.

2
00:00:02,505 --> 00:00:04,158
The collision variant.

3
00:00:05,283 --> 00:00:09,804
Diffusion needs hardware with r
nodes and a very precise readout.

4
00:00:10,184 --> 00:00:14,182
There is a purely digital
alternative that uses the same graph:

5
00:00:14,182 --> 00:00:18,243
launch random walkers and wait
for two to land on the same node.

6
00:00:18,623 --> 00:00:22,272
Each coincidence, or collision,
reveals a multiple of the order.

7
00:00:22,652 --> 00:00:26,382
Today we see how many samples are
needed and how the order is extracted.

8
00:00:27,483 --> 00:00:31,497
A word is a sequence of ± 2^t jumps.

9
00:00:31,877 --> 00:00:36,059
Its total exponent E is an
integer, and its endpoint is

10
00:00:36,059 --> 00:00:39,709
b^E mod N, computed with
modular exponentiation.

11
00:00:40,089 --> 00:00:45,529
If two words with different exponents
reach the same point, then b^D ≡

12
00:00:45,529 --> 00:00:50,192
1 with D the difference of
exponents: the order r divides D.

13
00:00:50,572 --> 00:00:52,537
A collision is a relation.

14
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:55,818
How many samples until
the first collision?

15
00:00:56,198 --> 00:01:01,030
It is the birthday paradox: with
T nearly uniform points in r

16
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:05,942
boxes, the expected number of
matching pairs is T(T − 1)/(2r),

17
00:01:06,322 --> 00:01:10,426
and the first collision appears
around √(π r/2) samples.

18
00:01:10,806 --> 00:01:14,829
We simulate it with N equal to one
million twenty-two thousand one

19
00:01:14,829 --> 00:01:18,791
hundred seventeen and order five
thousand three hundred thirteen:

20
00:01:19,171 --> 00:01:22,816
over a thousand repetitions,
the first collision arrives on

21
00:01:22,816 --> 00:01:26,214
average at sample 94.6,
against the prediction of 91.4.

22
00:01:27,317 --> 00:01:32,738
Each relation is D_i = q_i r
with an unknown multiplier.

23
00:01:33,118 --> 00:01:37,139
The running greatest common
divisor of the D_i is always a

24
00:01:37,139 --> 00:01:41,645
multiple of r, and it is exactly
r when the q_i share no factors.

25
00:01:42,025 --> 00:01:45,804
If they behave like random
integers, that happens with s

26
00:01:45,804 --> 00:01:50,123
relations with probability one
over zeta of s: sixty-one percent

27
00:01:50,123 --> 00:01:53,902
with two, eighty-three with
three, ninety-two with four.

28
00:01:54,282 --> 00:01:59,089
In the simulation, two relations
sufficed sixty percent of the time,

29
00:01:59,089 --> 00:02:03,189
three or fewer eighty-five,
and four or fewer ninety-four.

30
00:02:04,317 --> 00:02:08,073
This idea has a famous
relative: Pollard's rho method.

31
00:02:08,453 --> 00:02:13,873
Rho looks for collisions modulo
an unknown prime factor p of N, so

32
00:02:13,873 --> 00:02:18,718
the scale is √p, that is, of
order N^(1/4) for a semiprime,

33
00:02:19,098 --> 00:02:21,002
and it detects the collision with the

34
00:02:21,002 --> 00:02:23,215
greatest common divisor,
without knowing p.

35
00:02:23,595 --> 00:02:28,078
The variant in this lesson looks
for collisions of the order r,

36
00:02:28,078 --> 00:02:32,277
which can be as large as N:
the √r scale can be much worse.

37
00:02:33,383 --> 00:02:38,153
The treatise on diffusion-based
factoring takes this line further, with

38
00:02:38,153 --> 00:02:43,528
divisor-sensitive detectors that reach
the N^(1/4) scale with proven guarantees.

39
00:02:43,908 --> 00:02:47,583
But its conclusion is honest:
in the reproducible software

40
00:02:47,583 --> 00:02:51,448
comparison, Pollard with Brent's
improvement is still faster.

41
00:02:51,828 --> 00:02:55,038
Collisions are a useful
tool for understanding the

42
00:02:55,038 --> 00:02:58,054
structure, not a new way
to break cryptography.

43
00:02:59,183 --> 00:03:03,575
Birthday reasoning appears in
hash-function collisions and in

44
00:03:03,575 --> 00:03:08,183
low-memory cycle detection, such
as Floyd's and Brent's methods;

45
00:03:08,183 --> 00:03:12,143
the rho and kangaroo methods
solve discrete logarithms.

46
00:03:12,523 --> 00:03:17,053
In lab ten you will compare the
collision variant with Pollard and Brent.

47
00:03:18,183 --> 00:03:21,145
Common mistakes: counting
trivial collisions, with

48
00:03:21,145 --> 00:03:23,987
the same exponent, which
give a zero difference.

49
00:03:24,367 --> 00:03:28,335
Taking a single relation as the
order, when it is only a multiple.

50
00:03:28,715 --> 00:03:31,955
Assuming very short words
already give uniform points.

51
00:03:32,335 --> 00:03:36,062
And confusing the root-of-r scale
with Pollard's root-of-p scale.

52
00:03:37,183 --> 00:03:41,911
In summary: two words reaching the same
point give a multiple of the order.

53
00:03:42,291 --> 00:03:46,908
The first collision appears around
root-of-r samples, and the greatest common

54
00:03:46,908 --> 00:03:51,465
divisor of a few relations gives the
order, following the one-over-zeta law.

55
00:03:51,845 --> 00:03:56,373
It is the same principle as Pollard's
rho method, which in practice still wins.

56
00:03:57,483 --> 00:04:02,338
To practice: prove that a nontrivial
collision gives a multiple of the order.

57
00:04:02,718 --> 00:04:05,397
Compute the expected pairs
for one hundred samples.

58
00:04:05,777 --> 00:04:08,911
Compute the probability of
success with three relations.

59
00:04:09,291 --> 00:04:11,071
And explain Pollard's scale.
